  =\frac12-\frac12=0.$ Conversely, for two orthogonal states there exists a measurement that distinguishes them. Orthogonality is therefore not merely a geometric word; it has an operational meaning.

ja:
$\sigma_1$ の固有ベクトルは即座に確認を与える： $\braket{x+}{x-}
  =
  \frac12
  \begin{pmatrix}1&1\end{pmatrix}
  \begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix}
  =\frac12-\frac12=0.$ 逆に、二つの直交する状態に対しては、それらを区別する測定が存在する。したがって直交性は単なる幾何学的な言葉ではなく、操作的な意味を持つ。

zh:
$\sigma_1$本征向量提供了一个直接的检验： $\braket{x+}{x-}
  =
  \frac12
  \begin{pmatrix}1&1\end{pmatrix}
  \begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix}
  =\frac12-\frac12=0.$ 反过来，对于两个正交态，存在一种能够区分它们的测量。因此，正交性不仅仅是一个几何词汇；它具有操作上的意义。

p.src:
First prepare $\sigma_3=+1$: $\ket B=\ket{u}=\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\end{pmatrix}.$ Measure $\sigma_1$. For its $+1$ eigenvector, $\braket{x+}{u}
  =
  \frac{1}{\sqrt2}
  \begin{pmatrix}1&1\end{pmatrix}
  \begin{pmatrix}1\\0\end{pmatrix}
  =\frac{1}{\sqrt2},$ so $P(\sigma_1=+1\mid\sigma_3=+1)=\frac12.$ Replacing $\ket{x+}$ by $\ket{x-}$ gives the same probability. Reversing preparation and measurement also gives one half because the absolute square of the overlap is symmetric.

